NAME
       curl - transfer data from or to a URL (web pages, files, APIs)

SYNOPSIS
       curl [OPTION...] URL...

DESCRIPTION
       curl fetches each URL and writes what it gets to standard output. It
       speaks HTTP and HTTPS (and FTP and a few more). On the RHCSA it is the
       quickest way to check that a web server, a container's published port
       or a repository answers, and to download a file.

       By default curl shows a progress meter when output goes to a file or
       pipe; -s turns it off. It does not follow redirects unless you add -L.
       An HTTP error page (404, 403...) is still a successful transfer for
       curl, with exit status 0, unless you use -f.

OPTIONS
       -o FILE, --output FILE
              Save to FILE instead of standard output.

       -O, --remote-name
              Save to a file in the current directory named like the last part
              of the URL.

       -s, --silent
              No progress meter and no error messages.

       -S, --show-error
              With -s, still print error messages.

       -f, --fail
              Exit with status 22 (and no output) when the server answers
              with an HTTP error.

       -L, --location
              Follow redirects (3xx answers) to the new address.

       -I, --head
              Ask only for the headers and print them.

       -i, --include
              Print the response headers before the body.

       -v, --verbose
              Show the whole conversation: the request lines start with >,
              the answer's headers with <.

       -w FORMAT, --write-out FORMAT
              Print FORMAT after the transfer. %{http_code} is the status
              number, %{size_download} the bytes received, %{content_type} the
              type; \n is a new line.

       -k, --insecure
              Accept an HTTPS certificate that cannot be verified.

       -u USER:PASSWORD, --user USER:PASSWORD
              Log in with this name and password.

       -H "NAME: VALUE", --header "NAME: VALUE"
              Send an extra request header.

       -X METHOD, --request METHOD
              Use another HTTP method, such as PUT or DELETE.

       -d DATA, --data DATA
              Send DATA in a POST request.

       -m SECONDS, --max-time SECONDS
              Give up after this long.

       --connect-timeout SECONDS
              Give up connecting after this long.

EXAMPLES
       Check a web server on server2:

           curl http://server2.lab.example.com/

       Print only the HTTP status code (200 means OK):

           curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://server1:8080/

       Download a file into /root:

           curl -o /root/users.txt http://utility.lab.example.com/files/users.txt

       Look at the headers the server sends:

           curl -I http://repo.lab.example.com/BaseOS/

NOTES
       When curl prints "Connection refused", nothing listens on that port:
       check the service with systemctl status and ss -tlnp. "No route to
       host" from another machine usually means the firewall rejects the
       port. A 403 for a file that exists often means SELinux or the file
       permissions keep the web server out.

SEE ALSO
       wget(1), ss(8), firewall-cmd(1)
